  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Burnout Doesn't Announce Itself — Until It's Too Late",
      "body": "Most freelance designers don't see it coming. One day you love the work. Then you're dreading every brief. Here's how to stay ahead of it.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Set Working Hours and Actually Keep Them",
      "body": "Client availability creep is real. Define your hours, communicate them clearly, and stop treating every Slack ping as an emergency.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Cap the Number of Active Projects",
      "body": "Taking on more work feels like security. But juggling five clients at once drains creative energy fast. Set a hard limit and stick to it.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Charge Rates That Reflect Your Real Effort",
      "body": "Undercharging forces you to overwork. When your rate is too low, you compensate with volume — and volume is what burns you out.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Build Non-Billable Creative Time Into Your Week",
      "body": "Design work that has no client, no deadline, and no feedback loop. It refills the creative tank that client work quietly empties.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Learn to Recognize the Early Warning Signs",
      "body": "Irritability, slower output, dread before opening your laptop — these aren't laziness. They're signals. Catching them early changes everything.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save This and Share It With a Fellow Freelancer",
      "body": "Burnout is common in freelance design — but it's not inevitable. Save this carousel for your next rough patch and pass it on to someone who needs it.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Cash-Flow Forecasting for First-Time Agency Owners",
  "seriesTag": "Agency Finance 101",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Your agency looks profitable — and still can't make payroll.",
      "body": "Cash-flow gaps are the silent killer for new agencies. Here's how to forecast your way out of them before they hit.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "Revenue ≠ Cash in Hand",
      "body": "You booked a $20k project. Great. But if the client pays net-60, you're funding six weeks of salaries, software, and contractor invoices out of pocket first. Always map when money actually arrives, not when it's earned.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Build a 13-Week Rolling Forecast",
      "body": "Forget annual projections for now. Track every expected inflow and outflow week by week for the next 13 weeks. Update it every Monday. Thirteen weeks is close enough to act on and far enough to see trouble coming.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Know Your Three Cash Killers",
      "body": "1. Slow-paying clients on long net terms. 2. Contractor invoices due before client payment clears. 3. Uneven project starts — feast months followed by empty pipeline months. Label each one in your forecast so you can fix the right problem.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "The Retainer Fix Is Real — Here's Why",
      "body": "A single $5k monthly retainer doesn't just add revenue. It adds a predictable inflow date you can plan around. Even one or two retainers dramatically smooths the spikes that make forecasting feel impossible.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Set a Cash Reserve Rule on Day One",
      "body": "Keep a minimum cash buffer equal to six weeks of fixed costs — payroll, tools, rent. Treat that number as untouchable. If your forecast dips below it, that's your trigger to chase invoices, pause spending, or accelerate a proposal.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save this and open it next Monday morning.",
      "body": "Pull up your bank balance, list every payment due in and out over the next 13 weeks, and build your first forecast. One hour now will prevent a crisis later. Save this post so you don't lose the framework.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Motivation Is A Lie (Here's What Actually Works)",
  "seriesTag": "Habit Myths",
  "brand": {
    "apply": false,
    "paper": "#EFE9DA",
    "ink": "#212B21",
    "accent": "#C08A28",
    "headline": "editorial",
    "body": "clean"
  },
  "slides": [
    {
      "title": "Motivation Is A Lie (Here's What Actually Works)",
      "body": "Everyone says \"just stay motivated.\" But motivation is why most people quit. Here's the truth about building habits that actually stick.",
      "isCover": true
    },
    {
      "title": "MYTH: You Need To Feel Motivated To Start",
      "body": "Motivation follows action — it doesn't precede it. Waiting to feel ready is the fastest way to never begin. Consistency creates the feeling, not the other way around.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "MYTH: Motivated People Are More Disciplined",
      "body": "Highly consistent people don't rely on willpower. They design their environment so the habit becomes the path of least resistance. Systems beat self-control every time.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "MYTH: Low Motivation Means Something Is Wrong With You",
      "body": "Motivation naturally ebbs and flows — it's neurochemistry, not character. Treating it as a personal failure keeps you stuck in a shame spiral instead of a habit loop.",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "MYTH: Big Goals Keep You Motivated Long-Term",
      "body": "Big goals spike excitement and then fade. What keeps behavior going is a clear identity: \"I am someone who does this\" is more durable than \"I want to achieve that.\"",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "What Actually Builds Consistent Habits",
      "body": "→ Tiny starting points that feel almost too easy\n→ A fixed time and trigger (not a feeling)\n→ An identity tied to the behavior, not the outcome\n→ Tracking streaks, not chasing inspiration",
      "isCover": false
    },
    {
      "title": "Save This Before Your Motivation Runs Out",
      "body": "Share this with someone who keeps waiting to feel ready. Follow for more no-fluff habit science every week.",
      "isCover": true
    }
  ]
}

